# Avoid allocating a token substring when authenticating bearer tokens

- [x] You've read the [Contributor Guide](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md) and [Code of Conduct](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/CODE-OF-CONDUCT.md).
- [x] You've included unit or integration tests for your change, where applicable.
- [x] You've included inline docs for your change, where applicable.
- [ ] There's an open issue for the PR that you are making. If you'd like to propose a new feature or change, please open an issue to discuss the change or find an existing issue.

Unprotect the default bearer token directly from the `Authorization` header span.

## Description

`BearerTokenHandler.HandleAuthenticateAsync` runs on every request authenticated with `AddBearerToken`/`AddIdentityApiEndpoints`. It sliced the token out of the `Authorization` header with `authorization["Bearer ".Length..]`, which allocates a copy of the whole token (≈730 chars → ≈1.5 KB for a typical Identity access token) only so it can be passed to `ISecureDataFormat.Unprotect(string)`, which immediately Base64Url-decodes it into bytes.

**Change**
- `BearerTokenConfigureOptions` now creates the default `BearerTokenProtector` as a new internal `BearerTokenTicketDataFormat`, which derives from `TicketDataFormat` (so it is still a `TicketDataFormat`/`SecureDataFormat<AuthenticationTicket>`, with identical `Protect`/`Unprotect` behavior).
- It adds an internal `UnprotectSpan(ReadOnlySpan<char>)` that decodes with `Base64Url.DecodeFromChars` — the same call `WebEncoders.Base64UrlDecode` makes for Base64Url input — then unprotects and deserializes with `TicketSerializer.Default`, returning `null` on any failure exactly like `SecureDataFormat.Unprotect`.
- If the token contains `+` or `/` (i.e. it may be standard Base64, which `Base64UrlTextEncoder.Decode` also accepts), it falls back to the existing string path, so the set of accepted tokens is unchanged.
- The handler uses the span path only when `Options.BearerTokenProtector` is that default instance and the token comes from the header. Custom protectors and tokens provided via `OnMessageReceived` use the existing code path unchanged.
- No public API, token format, or response changes.

### Benchmark

Local BenchmarkDotNet (`MemoryDiagnoser`, default job), 3 separate process runs, both variants measured in each run. Each operation creates a DI scope and a `DefaultHttpContext` with an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header and calls `HttpContext.AuthenticateAsync("Identity.Bearer")`. The token holds a typical Identity principal (NameIdentifier, Name, Email, SecurityStamp, amr; 731 chars).

| Round | main Mean | PR Mean | main Alloc | PR Alloc |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 10.73 us ± 0.09 | 10.54 us ± 0.02 | 8.96 KB | 7.50 KB |
| 2 | 10.62 us ± 0.06 | 10.43 us ± 0.04 | 8.96 KB | 7.50 KB |
| 3 | 10.99 us ± 0.08 | 10.60 us ± 0.19 | 8.96 KB | 7.50 KB |

**−1.46 KB (−16%) allocated per bearer-authenticated request**, about 2–4% faster. The rest of the time is DataProtection and ticket deserialization. The saving grows with token size, for example with more claims.

<details><summary>Benchmark code</summary>

```csharp
[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class AuthBench
{
    IServiceProvider sp; IServiceScopeFactory sf; string header;

    [GlobalSetup]
    public void Setup()
    {
        var services = new ServiceCollection();
        services.AddLogging();
        services.AddDataProtection();
        services.AddAuthentication().AddBearerToken("Identity.Bearer");
        sp = services.BuildServiceProvider();
        sf = sp.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>();
        var opts = sp.GetRequiredService<IOptionsMonitor<BearerTokenOptions>>().Get("Identity.Bearer");
        var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal(new ClaimsIdentity(new[]
        {
            new Claim(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier, Guid.NewGuid().ToString()),
            new Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, "someone@example.com"),
            new Claim(ClaimTypes.Email, "someone@example.com"),
            new Claim("AspNet.Identity.SecurityStamp", "KJH3K4JH5K3J4H5K3J4H5K3J4H5K3J4H"),
            new Claim("amr", "pwd"),
        }, "Identity.Application"));
        var props = new AuthenticationProperties { ExpiresUtc = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddHours(1) };
        header = "Bearer " + opts.BearerTokenProtector.Protect(new AuthenticationTicket(principal, props, "Identity.Bearer:AccessToken"));
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public async Task<AuthenticateResult> Authenticate()
    {
        await using var scope = sf.CreateAsyncScope();
        var ctx = new DefaultHttpContext { RequestServices = scope.ServiceProvider };
        ctx.Request.Headers.Authorization = header;
        return await ctx.AuthenticateAsync("Identity.Bearer");
    }
}
```
The "PR" column used a copy of the handler and protector from this PR, registered as a second scheme in the same process.
</details>

