### Is there an existing issue for this?

I searched the open and closed issues and did not find one for this.

### Describe the bug

`IResponseCookies.Append` has three overloads. Two of them validate the cookie name and reject anything that is not an HTTP token. The third, the `ReadOnlySpan<KeyValuePair<string, string>>` batch overload, does not validate it at all, so the same name is rejected on two code paths and written straight into the `Set-Cookie` header on the third.

The two that validate reach `SetCookieHeaderValue.Name`, whose setter calls `CookieHeaderValue.CheckNameFormat`:

* `Append(string key, string value)` builds `new SetCookieHeaderValue(key, ...)` directly
* `Append(string key, string value, CookieOptions options)` goes through `CookieOptions.CreateCookieHeader`, which builds the same type

The batch overload instead concatenates the name into the header, in `ResponseCookies.cs`:

```csharp
cookies[position] = string.Concat(keyValuePair.Key, "=", Uri.EscapeDataString(keyValuePair.Value), cookieSuffix);
```

The value is escaped on all three paths. Only the name differs.

### Expected Behavior

All three overloads of the same interface method apply the same validation to the cookie name, so that moving from the single-cookie overload to the batch overload for performance does not silently drop a check.

### Steps To Reproduce

Minimal app, one endpoint per overload, passing the same name to each:

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

static string Key(HttpContext c) => c.Request.Query["k"].ToString();

app.MapGet("/two-arg", (HttpContext c) =>
{
    try { c.Response.Cookies.Append(Key(c), "VALUE"); return Results.Text("appended"); }
    catch (Exception e) { return Results.Text("THREW " + e.GetType().Name); }
});

app.MapGet("/three-arg", (HttpContext c) =>
{
    try { c.Response.Cookies.Append(Key(c), "VALUE", new CookieOptions { Path = "/" }); return Results.Text("appended"); }
    catch (Exception e) { return Results.Text("THREW " + e.GetType().Name); }
});

app.MapGet("/span", (HttpContext c) =>
{
    try
    {
        var pairs = new[] { new KeyValuePair<string, string>(Key(c), "VALUE") };
        c.Response.Cookies.Append(pairs, new CookieOptions { Path = "/" });
        return Results.Text("appended");
    }
    catch (Exception e) { return Results.Text("THREW " + e.GetType().Name); }
});

app.Run();
```

Request each route with `?k=` set to the name under test and look at the response headers. Observed on 10.0.14:

| name passed to `Append` | `two-arg` | `three-arg` | `span` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `sid` | `Set-Cookie: sid=VALUE; path=/` | same | same |
| `sid=OWNED; junk` | `ArgumentException` | `ArgumentException` | `Set-Cookie: sid=OWNED; junk=VALUE; path=/` |
| `sid; Domain=.evil.test` | `ArgumentException` | `ArgumentException` | `Set-Cookie: sid; Domain=.evil.test=VALUE; path=/` |
| `sid; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly` | `ArgumentException` | `ArgumentException` | written through unchanged |
| `sid name` | `ArgumentException` | `ArgumentException` | `Set-Cookie: sid name=VALUE; path=/` |
| `sid,other` | `ArgumentException` | `ArgumentException` | `Set-Cookie: sid,other=VALUE; path=/` |

For the second row a browser stores the cookie `sid` with the value `OWNED`, not the name and value the caller passed.

Carriage return and line feed are not a way out of the header: Kestrel rejects those later with `InvalidOperationException: Invalid non-ASCII or control character`, on all three paths. So this is about the cookie grammar, not about response splitting.

### Exceptions (if any)

On the two validating overloads:

```
System.ArgumentException: Invalid cookie name: sid=OWNED; junk (Parameter 'value')
```

The batch overload throws nothing and emits the header.

### .NET Version

10.0.14

### Anything else?

Also reproduces on 9.0.22 and 8.0.33.

Two call sites inside the framework use the unvalidated overload: `ChunkingCookieManager` and `ResponseCookiesWrapper` in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.CookiePolicy`. In that second file the single-cookie path and the batch path of the same wrapper disagree, since `Append(key, value)` and `Append(key, value, options)` validate and `Append(span, options)` does not. Neither of those passes a caller-controlled name today, so this reads to me as an API consistency problem rather than something exploitable on its own. I am happy to send a PR adding the same `CheckNameFormat` call to the batch overload if that is the direction you want, though it would be a behavioural change for anyone currently relying on names that the other overloads already reject.

